@using IBS.Services
@model IBSWeb.Areas.Filpride.Models.GlImportViewModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Import General Ledger";
}

<link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/index-style.css" asp-append-version="true" />

<div class="card shadow border-0 mt-4">
    <div class="card-header">
        <h2>@ViewData["Title"]</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Use this page to upload general ledger (GL) entries for your financial reports. Start with the Excel template and follow the steps below.</p>
        <h3 class="h5">1. Download the Excel template</h3>
        <p>The template includes a <strong>GL Upload</strong> sheet to fill in, an <strong>Instructions</strong> sheet, and lists of accounts, customers, and suppliers to copy from. Download a fresh template if those lists have changed.</p>
        <a asp-action="Template" class="btn btn-outline-secondary mb-3">Download Excel template</a>

        <h3 class="h5">2. Fill in the GL Upload sheet</h3>
        <p>Start at row 2. Keep all 13 column headings in row 1 exactly as they are and in the same order. Enter one debit or credit entry per row. Do not add total or subtotal rows.</p>
        <details class="mb-3" open>
            <summary class="fw-bold">Required columns: what to enter on every row</summary>
            <div class="table-responsive mt-2">
                <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Column in Excel</th><th>What to enter</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Date</td><td>The transaction date, written as year-month-day, for example <strong>2026-01-15</strong>.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Reference</td><td>The transaction or document number, for example <strong>JV-0001</strong> (up to 20 characters). Repeat it on all rows belonging to that transaction.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Description</td><td>A short explanation of the entry, for example <strong>Office supplies purchased</strong>.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>AccountNo / AccountName</td><td>Copy both values from the same row of <strong>Chart of Accounts</strong>. Keep the name's spelling and capital letters exactly as listed, and keep any zeros at the start of the account number.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Debit / Credit</td><td>Enter a positive or negative amount on one side and <strong>0</strong> on the other. Use numbers such as <strong>1000.00</strong> or <strong>-1000.00</strong>, without commas or currency symbols. Up to four decimal places are allowed.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Company</td><td>Type the company name on every row (up to 50 characters). Use the same spelling and capital letters each time. You do not need to register the company first.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>ModuleType</td><td>The transaction category (up to 50 characters), for example <strong>Sales</strong>, <strong>Purchase</strong>, <strong>Disbursement</strong>, <strong>DebitMemo</strong>, <strong>CreditMemo</strong>, <strong>Collection</strong>, or <strong>Journal</strong>. Other names are allowed. Use the same spelling and capital letters for the same category in every upload.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </details>
        <p><strong>Each transaction must balance:</strong> rows with the same Company, ModuleType, and Reference must have the same date, and their total debit must equal their total credit. For example, a debit of 1000.00 and a credit of 1000.00 on two rows balance one transaction. Balancing the file's overall totals alone is not enough.</p>
        <details class="mb-3">
            <summary class="fw-bold">Optional columns: customer or supplier details</summary>
            <p class="mt-2">Leave each pair below blank if it does not apply. If you use a pair, fill in both its type and ID. The system fills in the name for you.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>SubAccountType / SubAccountId:</strong> the customer or supplier whose individual ledger this entry belongs to. Find the name on <strong>Customers</strong> or <strong>Suppliers</strong>, then copy that row's <strong>SubAccountType</strong> and <strong>SubAccountId</strong>.</li>
                <li><strong>CounterpartyType / CounterpartyId:</strong> the customer or supplier involved in the transaction. For sales, use the customer; for purchases, use the supplier. Copy <strong>SubAccountType</strong> into <strong>CounterpartyType</strong> and <strong>SubAccountId</strong> into <strong>CounterpartyId</strong> from the reference sheet.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Use the number under <strong>SubAccountId</strong>, not CustomerCode or SupplierCode. The two pairs are separate and may refer to different records; filling in one does not fill in the other.</p>
            <p>For example, on a supplier purchase, the expense row can have its sub-account pair blank and its counterparty set to the supplier. The payable row can use that supplier in both pairs.</p>
        </details>

        <h3 class="h5">3. Save a CSV copy for uploading</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Save your completed Excel workbook so you can edit it later.</li>
            <li>Select the <strong>GL Upload</strong> sheet. In Excel, choose <strong>File &gt; Save As</strong> or <strong>Save a Copy</strong>.</li>
            <li>Choose the file type <strong>CSV UTF-8 (Comma delimited) (*.csv)</strong> and save. If Excel asks, choose to save only the active sheet and keep the CSV format.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Upload the new <strong>.csv</strong> file below. Keep your <strong>.xlsx</strong> workbook for corrections and reference.</p>

        <h3 class="h5">4. Check the preview, then confirm</h3>
        <p>Choose your CSV file and click <strong>Check file and preview</strong>. This checks your file without saving the entries. Review the dates, accounts, customer/supplier names, and amounts before confirming.</p>
        <div class="alert alert-warning">
            <strong>Uploading a correction?</strong> If Company, ModuleType, and Reference match a previously imported transaction, confirming replaces <strong>all</strong> its old entries with the rows in this file. Include every corrected row for that transaction, even rows that have not changed. Use the original spelling and capital letters for all three fields; changing any of them creates a separate transaction instead. The date may change. Old entries are permanently deleted and cannot be restored from import history. Transactions not included in the file stay unchanged. The same unchanged file cannot be imported twice.
        </div>
        <div asp-validation-summary="All" class="text-danger"></div>
        <form asp-action="Preview" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
            <label for="file" class="form-label">Choose your saved CSV file</label>
            <input id="file" name="file" type="file" accept=".csv" class="form-control" aria-describedby="file-help" required />
            <div id="file-help" class="form-text">Up to 10 MB and 50,000 entry rows. For larger files, keep all rows of each transaction together in the same file.</div>
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary mt-3">Check file and preview</button>
        </form>

        @if (Model.Preview is { } preview)
        {
            <hr />
            <h3>Preview: @preview.FileName</h3>
            <dl class="row">
                <dt class="col-sm-3">Valid lines</dt><dd class="col-sm-9">@preview.Rows.Count.ToString("N0")</dd>
                <dt class="col-sm-3">Transactions</dt><dd class="col-sm-9">@preview.JournalCount.ToString("N0")</dd>
                <dt class="col-sm-3">Total debit</dt><dd class="col-sm-9">@preview.TotalDebit.ToString("N4")</dd>
                <dt class="col-sm-3">Total credit</dt><dd class="col-sm-9">@preview.TotalCredit.ToString("N4")</dd>
            </dl>
            @if (preview.Errors.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="alert alert-danger">
                    <strong>No entries have been saved. Fix the items below in your Excel workbook, save the GL Upload sheet as CSV UTF-8 again, and upload the new CSV.</strong>
                    <ul>
                        @foreach (var error in preview.Errors)
                        {
                            <li>@error</li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                    @if (preview.Errors.Count == GlImportService.MaximumErrors)
                    {
                        <p>Showing the first @GlImportService.MaximumErrors errors.</p>
                    }
                </div>
            }
            <p>Showing up to 100 entry rows that passed the row checks. The entire file is checked before import; any error prevents the whole file from being imported. Row numbers refer to rows in your GL Upload sheet.</p>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-sm table-striped">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Row</th><th>Date</th><th>Reference</th><th>Description</th>
                            <th>AccountNo</th><th>AccountName</th>
                            <th class="text-end">Debit</th><th class="text-end">Credit</th>
                            <th>Sub-account type</th><th>Sub-account ID</th><th>Sub-account name</th>
                            <th>Company</th><th>Counterparty type</th><th>Counterparty ID</th><th>Counterparty name</th><th>Module type</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var row in preview.Rows.Take(100))
                        {
                            <tr>
                                <td>@row.RowNumber</td><td>@row.Date.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")</td>
                                <td>@row.Reference</td><td>@row.Description</td>
                                <td>@row.AccountNo</td><td>@row.AccountName</td>
                                <td class="text-end">@row.Debit.ToString("N4")</td>
                                <td class="text-end">@row.Credit.ToString("N4")</td>
                                <td>@row.SubAccountType</td><td>@row.SubAccountId</td><td>@row.SubAccountName</td>
                                <td>@row.Company</td><td>@row.CounterpartyType</td><td>@row.CounterpartyId</td><td>@row.CounterpartyName</td><td>@row.ModuleType</td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            @if (preview.CanImport && Model.PreviewToken != null)
            {
                <form asp-action="Import" method="post">
                    <input type="hidden" name="previewToken" value="@Model.PreviewToken" />
                    <p><strong>Your file passed the checks.</strong> Confirm within 15 minutes to save all entries in this file, including any replacements described above. If the preview expires, upload your CSV again.</p>
                    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-success">Confirm and save GL entries</button>
                </form>
            }
        }

        <hr />
        <h3>Import history</h3>
        <p>The latest 50 uploads are listed below. Counts and totals show each file as it was originally imported, even if some transactions were later replaced. History cannot restore replaced entries.</p>
        <p>After all entries are imported and checked, coordinate with <strong>MIS</strong> for month-end closing. MIS is responsible for locking and closing the months from earliest to latest. Reports that use closed balances need this step.</p>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm table-striped">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Batch</th><th>File</th><th>Imported by</th><th>Imported at</th><th>Period</th>
                        <th class="text-end">Lines</th><th class="text-end">Transactions</th>
                        <th class="text-end">Debit</th><th class="text-end">Credit</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var batch in Model.History)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>@batch.Id</td><td>@batch.FileName</td><td>@batch.ImportedBy</td>
                            <td>@batch.ImportedAt.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm")</td>
                            <td>@batch.DateFrom.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd") to @batch.DateTo.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")</td>
                            <td class="text-end">@batch.RowCount.ToString("N0")</td>
                            <td class="text-end">@batch.JournalCount.ToString("N0")</td>
                            <td class="text-end">@batch.TotalDebit.ToString("N4")</td>
                            <td class="text-end">@batch.TotalCredit.ToString("N4")</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
